$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">In the right triangle shown above, what is the length of&nbsp;<span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/13d9a1c3/img_001_bb3bd59c.png" alt="line P Q"></span></span>&nbsp;?</p>
$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="tcp-fd3ea050-58f4-4b16-a81d-baba01ecbcd1">The correct answer is 4. Triangle <span class="italic">PQR </span>has given angle measures of 30<!--StartFragment-->&deg;<!--EndFragment--> and 90<!--StartFragment-->&deg;<!--EndFragment-->, so the third angle must be 60&deg; because the measures of the angles of a triangle sum to 180<!--StartFragment-->&deg;<!--EndFragment-->. For any special right triangle with angles measuring 30&deg;, 60&deg;, and 90&deg;, the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is 2<span class="italic">x</span>, where <span class="italic">x</span> is the length of the side opposite the 30<!--StartFragment-->&deg;<!--EndFragment--> angle. Segment <span class="italic">PQ</span> is opposite the 30<!--StartFragment-->&deg;<!--EndFragment--> angle. Therefore, 2(<span class="italic">PQ</span>)<span class="italic"></span> = 8 and <span class="italic">PQ </span>= 4.</p>
