</li>
<li>A note indicates the figure is not drawn to scale.</li>
</ul></div></figure></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">In the figure, parallel lines <math alttext="q"><mi>q</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="t"><mi>t</mi>
</math> are intersected by lines <math alttext="r"><mi>r</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="s"><mi>s</mi>
</math>. If <math alttext="a equals 43"><mrow>
	<mi>a</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>43</mn>
</mrow>
</math> and <math alttext="b equals 122"><mrow>
	<mi>b</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>122</mn>
</mrow>
</math>, what is the value of <math alttext="w"><mi>w</mi>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["101/2","50.5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["101/2","50.5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math alttext="StartFraction 101 Over 2 EndFraction"><mfrac>
<mn>101</mn>
<mn>2</mn>
</mfrac>
</math>. In the figure, lines <math alttext="q"><mi>q</mi>
</math>, <math alttext="r"><mi>r</mi>
</math>, and <math alttext="s"><mi>s</mi>
</math> form a triangle. One interior angle of this triangle is vertical to the angle marked <math alttext="a degree"><mi>a</mi><mo>&deg;</mo></math>; therefore, the interior angle&nbsp;also has measure <math alttext="a degree"><mi>a</mi><mo>&deg;</mo></math>. It's given that <math alttext="a equals 43"><mrow>
<mi>a</mi>
<mo>=</mo>
<mn>43</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. Therefore, the interior angle of the triangle has measure&nbsp;<math alttext="43 degree"><mn>43</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>. A second interior angle of the triangle forms a straight line, <math alttext="q"><mi>q</mi>
</math>, with the angle marked <math alttext="b degree"><mi>b</mi><mo>&deg;</mo></math>. Therefore, the sum of the measures of these two angles is <math alttext="180 degree"><mn>180</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>. It's given that <math alttext="b equals 122"><mrow>
<mi>b</mi>
<mo>=</mo>
<mn>122</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. Therefore, the angle marked <math alttext="b degree"><mi>b</mi><mo>&deg;</mo></math> has measure <math alttext="122 degree"><mn>122</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math> and the second interior angle of the triangle has measure <math alttext="left parenthesis 180 minus 122 right parenthesis degree"><mfenced><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>122</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&deg;</mo></math>, or <math alttext="58 degree"><mn>58</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>. The sum of the interior angles of a triangle is&nbsp;<math alttext="180 degree"><mn>180</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>. Therefore, the measure of the third interior angle of the triangle is&nbsp;<math alttext="left parenthesis 180 minus 43 minus 58 right parenthesis degree"><mfenced><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>43</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>58</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&deg;</mo></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext="79 degree"><mn>79</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>. It's given that parallel lines <math alttext="q"><mi>q</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="t"><mi>t</mi>
</math> are intersected by line <math alttext="r"><mi>r</mi>
</math>. It follows that the triangle's interior angle with measure <math alttext="79 degree"><mn>79</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math> is congruent to the same side interior angle between lines <math alttext="q"><mi>q</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="t"><mi>t</mi>
</math> formed by lines <math alttext="t"><mi>t</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="r"><mi>r</mi>
</math>. Since this angle is supplementary to the two angles marked <math alttext="w degree"><mi>w</mi><mo>&deg;</mo></math>, the sum of&nbsp;<math alttext="79 degree"><mn>79</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>,&nbsp;<math alttext="w degree"><mi>w</mi><mo>&deg;</mo></math>, and&nbsp;<math alttext="w degree"><mi>w</mi><mo>&deg;</mo></math> is&nbsp;<math alttext="180 degree"><mn>180</mn><mo>&deg;</mo></math>. It follows that&nbsp;<math alttext="79 plus w plus w equals 180"><mn>79</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext="79 plus 2 w equals 180"><mn>79</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn></math>. Subtracting <math alttext="79"><mn>79</mn>
</math> from both sides of this equation yields&nbsp;<math alttext="2 w equals 101"><mn>2</mn><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>101</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext="2"><mn>2</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="w equals StartFraction 101 Over 2 EndFraction"><mrow>
<mi>w</mi>
<mo>=</mo>
<mfrac>
<mn>101</mn>
<mn>2</mn>
</mfrac>
</mrow>
