</g>\n   <g id=\"line2d_2\">\n    <path clip-path=\"url(#p483cabe76b)\" d=\"M 108.006896 144.306581 \nL 108.427399 139.530231 \nL 109.268405 135.661338 \nL 110.109412 133.04285 \nL 111.370921 130.037358 \nL 112.63243 127.634814 \nL 114.314443 124.997366 \nL 115.996455 122.797239 \nL 117.678468 120.914063 \nL 119.780983 118.901989 \nL 121.883499 117.193329 \nL 123.986014 115.734805 \nL 126.509033 114.264932 \nL 129.032052 113.061286 \nL 131.555071 112.094597 \nL 134.078089 111.344414 \nL 136.601108 110.796468 \nL 139.54463 110.400163 \nL 142.488152 110.257175 \nL 145.431673 110.364361 \nL 148.375195 110.724072 \nL 150.898214 111.239357 \nL 153.421232 111.954983 \nL 155.944251 112.884359 \nL 158.46727 114.046762 \nL 160.990289 115.469738 \nL 163.092804 116.882957 \nL 165.19532 118.538019 \nL 167.297835 120.483945 \nL 168.979848 122.300046 \nL 170.66186 124.412301 \nL 172.343873 126.924499 \nL 173.605382 129.184541 \nL 174.866891 131.949923 \nL 175.707898 134.263288 \nL 176.548904 137.334145 \nL 176.969407 139.530231 \nL 177.38991 144.306581 \nL 177.38991 144.306581 \n\" style=\"fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-linecap:square;stroke-width:2.3;\"></path>\n   </g>\n  </g>\n </g>\n <defs>\n  <clipPath id=\"p483cabe76b\">\n   <rect height=\"260.82\" width=\"268.898496\" x=\"7.2\" y=\"7.2\"></rect>\n  </clipPath>\n </defs>\n</svg>\n<div role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for graph of a circle\" class=\"sr-only\"><ul><li>The circle passes through the following approximate points:<br>\n<ul>\n<li>(0 comma 8.6)</li>\n<li>(2.6 comma 6)</li>\n<li>(0 comma 3.4)</li>\n<li>(negative 2.6 comma 6)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul></div></figure></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Circle A shown is defined by the equation&nbsp;<math alttext=\"x squared plus left parenthesis y minus 6 right parenthesis squared equals 7\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></math>. Circle B (not shown) has the same radius but is translated <math alttext=\"96\"><mn>96</mn>\n</math> units to the right. If the equation of circle B is <math alttext=\"left parenthesis x minus h right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis y minus k right parenthesis squared equals a\"><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></math>, where <math alttext=\"h\"><mi>h</mi>\n</math>, <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math>, and <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math> are constants, what is the value of <math alttext=\"4 a\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>4</mn>\n\t<mi>a</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>?</p>","keys":["28"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"28\"><mn>28</mn></math>. The equation of a circle in the <em>xy</em>-plane can be written as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis x minus t right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis y minus s right parenthesis squared equals r squared\"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, where the center of the circle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis t comma s right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></math> and the radius of the circle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math>. It&rsquo;s given that circle A is defined by the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x squared plus left parenthesis y minus 6 right parenthesis squared equals 7\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></math>, which can be written as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis x minus 0 right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis y minus 6 right parenthesis squared equals left parenthesis StartRoot 7 EndRoot right parenthesis squared\"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mfenced><msqrt><mn>7</mn></msqrt></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. It follows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r equals StartRoot 7 EndRoot\"><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>7</mn></msqrt></math> and the radius of circle A is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartRoot 7 EndRoot\"><msqrt><mn>7</mn></msqrt></math>. It&rsquo;s also given that circle B has the same radius as circle A. If the equation of circle B is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis x minus h right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis y minus k right parenthesis squared equals a\"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a equals r squared\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartRoot 7 EndRoot\"><msqrt><mn>7</mn></msqrt></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> in this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a equals left parenthesis StartRoot 7 EndRoot right parenthesis squared\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mfenced><msqrt><mn>7</mn></msqrt></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a equals 7\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></math>. It follows that the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"4 a\"><mn>4</mn><mi>a</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"4 left parenthesis 7 right parenthesis\"><mn>4</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"28\"><mn>28</mn></math>.</p>","externalid":"a60b410d-c043-4ee9-8579-74c1c9e9aad2","correct_answer":["28"]},"createDate":1755117017721,"updateDate":1755117017721}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117017721, 1755117017721),
    ($SATQ$1be8b6b2$SATQ$, $SATQ$aa19a0c6-76f9-4d66-b105-5e444ac3faec$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Area and volume$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">A right circular cone has a volume of <math alttext="71,148 pi"><mn>71,148</mn>
<mi>&#960;</mi></math> cubic centimeters and the area of its base is <math alttext="5,929 pi"><mn>5,929</mn>
<mi>&#960;</mi></math> square centimeters. What is the slant height, in centimeters, of this cone?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"fe6f3306-46c9-4c0e-8e16-03e1195f00bc","content":"<p><math alttext=\"12\"><mn>12</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"14a28403-d777-4b12-a424-cccb9db95c45","content":"<p><math alttext=\"36\"><mn>36</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"c6677c71-0a3c-4d30-8d5c-5c1e4eaf3488","content":"<p><math alttext=\"77\"><mn>77</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"67fc5222-0d00-4369-a487-25c140c69cf5","content":"<p><math alttext=\"85\"><mn>85</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["67fc5222-0d00-4369-a487-25c140c69cf5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is correct. The volume, <math alttext="upper V"><mi>V</mi>
</math>, of a right circular cone is given by the formula <math alttext="upper V equals one third pi r squared h"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>&pi;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math>, where <math alttext="pi r squared"><mrow>
<mi>&pi;</mi>
<msup>
<mi>r</mi>
<mn>2</mn>
</msup>
</mrow>
</math> is the area of the circular base of the cone and <math alttext="h"><mi>h</mi>
</math> is the height. It&rsquo;s given that this right circular cone has a volume of <math alttext="71,148 pi"><mrow>
<mn>71,148</mn>
<mi>&pi;</mi>
</mrow>
</math> cubic centimeters and the area of its base is <math alttext="5,929 pi"><mrow>
<mn>5,929</mn>
<mi>&pi;</mi>
</mrow>
</math> square centimeters. Substituting <math alttext="71,148 pi"><mrow>
<mn>71,148</mn>
<mi>&pi;</mi>
</mrow>
</math> for <math alttext="upper V"><mi>V</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="5,929 pi"><mrow>
<mn>5,929</mn>
<mi>&pi;</mi>
</mrow>
</math> for <math alttext="pi r squared"><mrow>
<mi>&pi;</mi>
<msup>
<mi>r</mi>
<mn>2</mn>
</msup>
</mrow>
</math> in the formula <math alttext="upper V equals one third pi r squared h"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>&pi;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math> yields <math alttext="71,148 pi equals left parenthesis one third right parenthesis left parenthesis 5,929 pi right parenthesis left parenthesis h right parenthesis"><mn>71,148</mn><mi>&pi;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mfenced><mrow><mn>5,929</mn><mi>&pi;</mi></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mi>h</mi></mfenced></math>. Dividing each side of this equation by <math alttext="5,929 pi"><mrow>
<mn>5,929</mn>
<mi>&pi;</mi>
</mrow>
</math> yields <math alttext="12 equals StartFraction h Over 3 EndFraction"><mrow>
<mn>12</mn>
<mo>=</mo>
<mrow>
<mfrac>
<mi>h</mi>
<mn>3</mn>
</mfrac>
</mrow>
</mrow>
</math>. Multiplying each side of this equation by <math alttext="3"><mn>3</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="36 equals h"><mrow>
<mn>36</mn>
<mo>=</mo>
<mi>h</mi>
</mrow>
</math>. Let <math alttext="s"><mi>s</mi>
</math> represent the slant height, in centimeters, of this cone. A right triangle is formed by the radius, <math alttext="r"><mi>r</mi>
</math>, height, <math alttext="h"><mi>h</mi>
</math>, and slant height, <math alttext="s"><mi>s</mi>
</math>, of this cone, where <math alttext="r"><mi>r</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="h"><mi>h</mi>
</math> are the legs of the triangle and <math alttext="s"><mi>s</mi>
</math> is the hypotenuse. Using the Pythagorean theorem, the equation <math alttext="r squared plus h squared equals s squared"><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> represents this relationship. Because <math alttext="5,929 pi"><mrow>
<mn>5,929</mn>
<mi>&pi;</mi>
</mrow>
</math> is the area of the base and the area of the base is <math alttext="pi r squared"><mrow>
<mi>&pi;</mi>
<msup>
<mi>r</mi>
<mn>2</mn>
</msup>
</mrow>
</math>, it follows that <math alttext="5,929 pi equals pi r squared"><mrow>
<mrow>
<mn>5,929</mn>
<mi>&pi;</mi>
</mrow>
<mo>=</mo>
<mrow>
<mi>&pi;</mi>
<msup>
<mi>r</mi>
<mn>2</mn>
</msup>
</mrow>
</mrow>
</math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext="pi"><mi>&pi;</mi>
</math> yields <math alttext="5,929 equals r squared"><mrow>
<mn>5,929</mn>
<mo>=</mo>
<msup>
<mi>r</mi>
<mn>2</mn>
</msup>
</mrow>
</math>. Substituting <math alttext="5,929"><mn>5,929</mn>
</math> for <math alttext="r squared"><msup>
<mi>r</mi>
<mn>2</mn>
</msup>
</math> and <math alttext="36"><mn>36</mn>
</math> for <math alttext="h"><mi>h</mi>
</math> in the equation <math alttext="r squared plus h squared equals s squared"><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> yields <math alttext="5,929 plus 36 squared equals s squared"><mn>5,929</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>36</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, which is equivalent to <math alttext="5,929 plus 1,296 equals s squared"><mn>5,929</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1,296</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math alttext="7,225 equals s squared"><mrow>
