$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">Triangle <span class="italic">ABC</span> and triangle <span class="italic">DEF</span> are shown. The relationship between the side lengths of the two triangles is such that <span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/1c3d613c/img_001_b81704a1.png" alt="the length of side A, B, over, the length of side D E, equals, the length of side B C, over, the length of side E F, which equals, the length of side A, C, over, the length of side D F, which equals 3"></span></span>. If the measure of angle <span class="italic">BAC</span> is 20&deg;, what is the measure, in degrees, of angle <span class="italic">EDF</span> ? (Disregard the degree symbol when gridding your answer.)</p>
$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["20"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>The correct answer is 20. By the equality given, the three pairs of corresponding sides of the two triangles are in the same proportion. By the side-side-side (SSS) similarity theorem, triangle<span class="italic"> ABC</span> is similar to triangle <span class="italic">DEF</span>. In similar triangles, the measures of corresponding angles are congruent. Since angle <span class="italic">BAC</span> corresponds to angle <span class="italic">EDF</span>, these two angles are congruent and their measures are equal. It&rsquo;s given that the measure of angle<span class="italic"> BAC </span>is 20&deg;, so the measure of angle <span class="italic">ED</span><span class="italic">F</span> is also 20&deg;.</p>
