	<mi>T</mi>
</mrow>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0e4a676f-2102-40b5-99f5-9bdb6faa99e2","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper F\"><mi>F</mi>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"24b37819-8b74-4656-8495-d0fad59b6765","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper T\"><mi>T</mi>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"e4daa1d5-95af-4b9e-86f5-f9bc98ce141b","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper F\"><mi>F</mi>\n</math> and the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper T\"><mi>T</mi>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"e73f2dbb-e6df-4d07-8369-8f108340c69f","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">No additional information is needed.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["e73f2dbb-e6df-4d07-8369-8f108340c69f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is correct. When two angles of one triangle are congruent to two angles of another triangle, the triangles are similar. It's given that in triangle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper D upper E upper F"><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi></math>, the measure of angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper D"><mi>D</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="47 degree"><mn>47</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and the measure of angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper E"><mi>E</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="97 degree"><mn>97</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It's also given that in triangle&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper R upper S upper T"><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>T</mi></math>, the measure of angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper R"><mi>R</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="47 degree"><mn>47</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and the measure of angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper S"><mi>S</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="97 degree"><mn>97</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It follows that angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper D"><mi>D</mi></math> is congruent to angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper R"><mi>R</mi></math> and that angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper E"><mi>E</mi></math> is congruent to angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper S"><mi>S</mi></math>. Therefore, triangle&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper D upper E upper F"><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi></math>&nbsp;is similar to triangle&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper R upper S upper T"><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>T</mi></math>&nbsp;and no additional information is needed.</p>
<p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>
<p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>
<p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2085e10e","external_id":"c58fbfdc-93b3-4cba-83da-ffc2c85dd427","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"63cd5521-4669-46cb-a347-3c802e65f599","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.B.","skill_desc":"Lines, angles, and triangles","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In triangle <math alttext=\"upper D upper E upper F\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t<mi>F</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper D\"><mi>D</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"47 degree\"><mrow><mn>47</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"97 degree\"><mrow><mn>97</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. In triangle <math alttext=\"upper R upper S upper T\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n\t<mi>S</mi>\n\t<mi>T</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"47 degree\"><mrow><mn>47</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper S\"><mi>S</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"97 degree\"><mrow><mn>97</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Which of the following additional pieces of information is needed to determine whether triangle <math alttext=\"upper D upper E upper F\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t<mi>F</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is similar to triangle <math alttext=\"upper R upper S upper T\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n\t<mi>S</mi>\n\t<mi>T</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0e4a676f-2102-40b5-99f5-9bdb6faa99e2","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper F\"><mi>F</mi>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"24b37819-8b74-4656-8495-d0fad59b6765","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper T\"><mi>T</mi>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"e4daa1d5-95af-4b9e-86f5-f9bc98ce141b","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper F\"><mi>F</mi>\n</math> and the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper T\"><mi>T</mi>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"e73f2dbb-e6df-4d07-8369-8f108340c69f","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">No additional information is needed.</p>"}],"keys":["e73f2dbb-e6df-4d07-8369-8f108340c69f"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. When two angles of one triangle are congruent to two angles of another triangle, the triangles are similar. It's given that in triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper D upper E upper F\"><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi></math>, the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper D\"><mi>D</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"47 degree\"><mn>47</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"97 degree\"><mn>97</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It's also given that in triangle&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R upper S upper T\"><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>T</mi></math>, the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"47 degree\"><mn>47</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper S\"><mi>S</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"97 degree\"><mn>97</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It follows that angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper D\"><mi>D</mi></math> is congruent to angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R\"><mi>R</mi></math> and that angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi></math> is congruent to angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper S\"><mi>S</mi></math>. Therefore, triangle&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper D upper E upper F\"><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi></math>&nbsp;is similar to triangle&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper R upper S upper T\"><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>T</mi></math>&nbsp;and no additional information is needed.</p>\n<p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>\n<p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>\n<p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1755117017723,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"2085e10e","skill_cd":"S.B.","score_band_range_cd":5,"skill_desc":"Lines, angles, and triangles","createDate":1755117017723,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"","external_id":"c58fbfdc-93b3-4cba-83da-ffc2c85dd427","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"63cd5521-4669-46cb-a347-3c802e65f599","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"type":"mcq","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In triangle <math alttext=\"upper D upper E upper F\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t<mi>F</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper D\"><mi>D</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"47 degree\"><mrow><mn>47</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"97 degree\"><mrow><mn>97</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. In triangle <math alttext=\"upper R upper S upper T\">