 36, equals 9\"></span>.<p>Choices B, C, and D are incorrect. The perpendicular diameters divide the circumference of the circle into four equal arcs; therefore, minor arc <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/23c5fcce/img_010_1b7444d1.png\" alt=\"A, C\"></span> is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/23c5fcce/img_011_929b687b.png\" alt=\"one fourth\"></span> of the circumference. However, the lengths in choices B and C are, respectively, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/23c5fcce/img_012_7445a232.png\" alt=\"one third\"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/23c5fcce/img_013_6b62f681.png\" alt=\"one half\"></span> the circumference of the circle, and the length in choice D is the length of the entire circumference. None of these lengths is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/23c5fcce/img_014_929b687b.png\" alt=\"one fourth\"></span> the circumference.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":15,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959635,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"23c5fcce","skill_cd":"S.D.","score_band_range_cd":3,"skill_desc":"Circles","createDate":1691007959635,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"08358-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"95c2ee68-09ce-4d8a-978f-d143eb3e17e0","difficulty":"E"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"08358-DC","section":"Math","body":"<div class=\"stimulus_reference \" xmlns=\"http://www.imsglobal.org/xsd/apip/apipv1p0/qtiitem/imsqti_v2p1\">\n\t<div class=\"standalone_image \"><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"The figure presents a circle with center O. Points A, and C are indicated on the circle, creating minor arc A, C. A diameter is drawn from point A to a point on the other side of the circle. Similarly, a diameter is drawn from point C to a point on the other side of the circle. The two lines intersect at the origin, forming a right angle at angle A, O C\" width=\"82\" height=\"82\"></div>\n</div>\n","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">The circle above with center <span class=\"italic\">O</span> has a circumference of 36. What is the length of minor arc <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"A, C\"></span>?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p><span class=\"phantom_text \">9</span></p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p>12</p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p>18</p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p>36</p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"a","rationale":"<p>Choice A is correct. A circle has 360 degrees of arc. In the circle shown, <span class=\"italic\">O</span> is the center of the circle and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"angle A, O C\"></span> is a central angle of the circle. From the figure, the two diameters that meet to form <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"angle A, O C\"></span> are perpendicular, so the measure of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"angle A, O C\"></span> is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"90 degrees\"></span>. Therefore, the length of minor arc <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"A, C\"></span> is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction 90 over 360\"></span> of the circumference of the circle. Since the circumference of the circle is 36, the length of minor arc <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"A, C\"></span> is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction 90 over 360, end fraction, times 36, equals 9\"></span>.<p>Choices B, C, and D are incorrect. The perpendicular diameters divide the circumference of the circle into four equal arcs; therefore, minor arc <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"A, C\"></span> is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one fourth\"></span> of the circumference. However, the lengths in choices B and C are, respectively, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one third\"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one half\"></span> the circumference of the circle, and the length in choice D is the length of the entire circumference. None of these lengths is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one fourth\"></span> the circumference.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959635,"updateDate":1691007959635}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959635, 1691007959635),
    ($SATQ$244ff6c4$SATQ$, $SATQ$97ba1ce7-b7c6-4164-bd91-cbae30d8e22a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Circles$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">What is the value of&nbsp;<math alttext="tangent StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction"><mi>tan</mi><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>92</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0defc59d-166e-466b-9963-42e573da70f8","content":"<p><math alttext=\"minus StartRoot 3 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"2784335c-c06d-43cc-adc6-18473b7db44a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"minus StartFraction StartRoot 3 EndRoot Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"107d172c-1193-4845-a939-1d51b1bdcf21","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction StartRoot 3 EndRoot Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"91a69216-847a-4163-a9d9-7407154f02af","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartRoot 3 EndRoot\"><msqrt>\n\t<mn>3</mn>\n</msqrt>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0defc59d-166e-466b-9963-42e573da70f8"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is correct. A trigonometric ratio can be found using the unit circle, that is, a circle with radius <math alttext="1"><mn>1</mn>
</math> unit. If a central angle of a unit circle in the <em>xy</em>-plane centered at the origin has its starting side on the positive <em>x</em>-axis and its terminal side intersects the circle at a point <math alttext="left parenthesis x comma y right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>, then the value of the tangent of the central angle is equal to the <em>y</em>-coordinate divided by the <em>x</em>-coordinate. There are <math alttext="2 pi"><mrow>
	<mn>2</mn>
	<mi>&pi;</mi>
</mrow>
</math> radians in a circle. Dividing <math alttext="StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction"><mrow>
	<mfrac>
		<mrow>
			<mn>92</mn>
			<mi>&pi;</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mn>3</mn>
	</mfrac>
</mrow>
</math> by <math alttext="2 pi"><mrow>
	<mn>2</mn>
	<mi>&pi;</mi>
</mrow>
</math> yields <math alttext="StartFraction 92 Over 6 EndFraction"><mfrac><mn>92</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac></math>, which is equivalent to <math alttext="15 plus two thirds"><mn>15</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>. It follows that on the unit circle centered at the origin in the <em>xy</em>-plane, the angle <math alttext="StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction"><mrow>
	<mfrac>
		<mrow>
			<mn>92</mn>
			<mi>&pi;</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mn>3</mn>
	</mfrac>
</mrow>
</math> is the result of <math alttext="15"><mn>15</mn>
</math> revolutions from its starting side on the positive <em>x</em>-axis followed by a rotation through <math alttext="StartFraction 2 pi Over 3 EndFraction"><mrow>
	<mfrac>
		<mrow>
			<mn>2</mn>
			<mi>&pi;</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mn>3</mn>
	</mfrac>
</mrow>
</math> radians. Therefore, the angles <math alttext="StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction"><mrow>
	<mfrac>
		<mrow>
			<mn>92</mn>
			<mi>&pi;</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mn>3</mn>
	</mfrac>
</mrow>
</math> and <math alttext="StartFraction 2 pi Over 3 EndFraction"><mrow>
	<mfrac>
		<mrow>
			<mn>2</mn>
			<mi>&pi;</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mn>3</mn>
	</mfrac>
</mrow>
</math> are coterminal angles and <math alttext="tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math> is equal to <math alttext="tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 2 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>. Since <math alttext="StartFraction 2 pi Over 3 EndFraction"><mrow>
	<mfrac>
		<mrow>
			<mn>2</mn>
			<mi>&pi;</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mn>3</mn>
	</mfrac>
</mrow>
</math> is greater than <math alttext="StartFraction pi Over 2 EndFraction"><mrow>
	<mfrac>
		<mi>&pi;</mi>
		<mn>2</mn>
	</mfrac>
</mrow>
</math> and less than <math alttext="pi"><mi>&pi;</mi>
</math>, it follows that the terminal side of the angle is in quadrant II and forms an angle of <math alttext="StartFraction pi Over 3 EndFraction"><mrow>
	<mfrac>
		<mi>&pi;</mi>
		<mn>3</mn>
	</mfrac>
</mrow>
</math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext="60 degree"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, with the negative <em>x</em>-axis. Therefore, the terminal side of the angle intersects the unit circle at the point <math alttext="left parenthesis negative one half comma StartFraction StartRoot 3 EndRoot Over 2 EndFraction right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math>. It follows that&nbsp;the value of <math alttext="tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 2 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math> is&nbsp;<math alttext="StartStartFraction StartFraction StartRoot 3 EndRoot Over 2 EndFraction OverOver negative one half EndEndFraction"><mfrac><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mo>-</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mfrac></math>, which is equivalent to <math alttext="minus StartRoot 3 EndRoot"><mrow>
	<mo>-</mo>
	<msqrt>
		<mn>3</mn>
	</msqrt>
</mrow>
</math>. Therefore, the value of <math alttext="tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math> is <math alttext="minus StartRoot 3 EndRoot"><mrow>
	<mo>-</mo>
	<msqrt>
		<mn>3</mn>
	</msqrt>
</mrow>
</math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect. This is the value of <math alttext="StartStartFraction 1 OverOver tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis EndEndFraction"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></mrow></mfrac></math>, not <math alttext="tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect. This is the value of <math alttext="StartStartFraction 1 OverOver tangent left parenthesis StartFraction pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis EndEndFraction"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></mrow></mfrac></math>, not <math alttext="tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect. This is the value of <math alttext="tangent left parenthesis StartFraction pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>, not <math alttext="tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"244ff6c4","external_id":"97ba1ce7-b7c6-4164-bd91-cbae30d8e22a","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"99c167b3-f443-4d64-ad11-279a2ad1a609","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.D.","skill_desc":"Circles","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">What is the value of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"tangent StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>92</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0defc59d-166e-466b-9963-42e573da70f8","content":"<p><math alttext=\"minus StartRoot 3 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"2784335c-c06d-43cc-adc6-18473b7db44a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"minus StartFraction StartRoot 3 EndRoot Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"107d172c-1193-4845-a939-1d51b1bdcf21","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction StartRoot 3 EndRoot Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"91a69216-847a-4163-a9d9-7407154f02af","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartRoot 3 EndRoot\"><msqrt>\n\t<mn>3</mn>\n</msqrt>\n</math></p>"}],"keys":["0defc59d-166e-466b-9963-42e573da70f8"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is correct. A trigonometric ratio can be found using the unit circle, that is, a circle with radius <math alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn>\n</math> unit. If a central angle of a unit circle in the <em>xy</em>-plane centered at the origin has its starting side on the positive <em>x</em>-axis and its terminal side intersects the circle at a point <math alttext=\"left parenthesis x comma y right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>, then the value of the tangent of the central angle is equal to the <em>y</em>-coordinate divided by the <em>x</em>-coordinate. There are <math alttext=\"2 pi\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> radians in a circle. Dividing <math alttext=\"StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>92</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math> by <math alttext=\"2 pi\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"StartFraction 92 Over 6 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>92</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac></math>, which is equivalent to <math alttext=\"15 plus two thirds\"><mn>15</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>. It follows that on the unit circle centered at the origin in the <em>xy</em>-plane, the angle <math alttext=\"StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>92</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math> is the result of <math alttext=\"15\"><mn>15</mn>\n</math> revolutions from its starting side on the positive <em>x</em>-axis followed by a rotation through <math alttext=\"StartFraction 2 pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math> radians. Therefore, the angles <math alttext=\"StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>92</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"StartFraction 2 pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math> are coterminal angles and <math alttext=\"tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math> is equal to <math alttext=\"tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 2 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>. Since <math alttext=\"StartFraction 2 pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math> is greater than <math alttext=\"StartFraction pi Over 2 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math> and less than <math alttext=\"pi\"><mi>&pi;</mi>\n</math>, it follows that the terminal side of the angle is in quadrant II and forms an angle of <math alttext=\"StartFraction pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext=\"60 degree\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, with the negative <em>x</em>-axis. Therefore, the terminal side of the angle intersects the unit circle at the point <math alttext=\"left parenthesis negative one half comma StartFraction StartRoot 3 EndRoot Over 2 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math>. It follows that&nbsp;the value of <math alttext=\"tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 2 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math> is&nbsp;<math alttext=\"StartStartFraction StartFraction StartRoot 3 EndRoot Over 2 EndFraction OverOver negative one half EndEndFraction\"><mfrac><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mo>-</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mfrac></math>, which is equivalent to <math alttext=\"minus StartRoot 3 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Therefore, the value of <math alttext=\"tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math> is <math alttext=\"minus StartRoot 3 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect. This is the value of <math alttext=\"StartStartFraction 1 OverOver tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis EndEndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></mrow></mfrac></math>, not <math alttext=\"tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. This is the value of <math alttext=\"StartStartFraction 1 OverOver tangent left parenthesis StartFraction pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis EndEndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></mrow></mfrac></math>, not <math alttext=\"tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. This is the value of <math alttext=\"tangent left parenthesis StartFraction pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>, not <math alttext=\"tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1730147357408,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"244ff6c4","skill_cd":"S.D.","score_band_range_cd":6,"skill_desc":"Circles","createDate":1730147357408,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"","external_id":"97ba1ce7-b7c6-4164-bd91-cbae30d8e22a","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"99c167b3-f443-4d64-ad11-279a2ad1a609","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"answerOptions":[{"content":"<p><math alttext=\"minus StartRoot 3 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>","id":"0defc59d-166e-466b-9963-42e573da70f8"},{"content":"<p><math alttext=\"minus StartFraction StartRoot 3 EndRoot Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>","id":"2784335c-c06d-43cc-adc6-18473b7db44a"},{"content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction StartRoot 3 EndRoot Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>","id":"107d172c-1193-4845-a939-1d51b1bdcf21"},{"content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartRoot 3 EndRoot\"><msqrt>\n\t<mn>3</mn>\n</msqrt>\n</math></p>","id":"91a69216-847a-4163-a9d9-7407154f02af"}],"externalid":"97ba1ce7-b7c6-4164-bd91-cbae30d8e22a","keys":["0defc59d-166e-466b-9963-42e573da70f8"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is correct. A trigonometric ratio can be found using the unit circle, that is, a circle with radius <math alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn>\n</math> unit. If a central angle of a unit circle in the <em>xy</em>-plane centered at the origin has its starting side on the positive <em>x</em>-axis and its terminal side intersects the circle at a point <math alttext=\"left parenthesis x comma y right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>, then the value of the tangent of the central angle is equal to the <em>y</em>-coordinate divided by the <em>x</em>-coordinate. There are <math alttext=\"2 pi\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> radians in a circle. Dividing <math alttext=\"StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>92</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math> by <math alttext=\"2 pi\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"StartFraction 92 Over 6 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>92</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac></math>, which is equivalent to <math alttext=\"15 plus two thirds\"><mn>15</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>. It follows that on the unit circle centered at the origin in the <em>xy</em>-plane, the angle <math alttext=\"StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>92</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math> is the result of <math alttext=\"15\"><mn>15</mn>\n</math> revolutions from its starting side on the positive <em>x</em>-axis followed by a rotation through <math alttext=\"StartFraction 2 pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math> radians. Therefore, the angles <math alttext=\"StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>92</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"StartFraction 2 pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math> are coterminal angles and <math alttext=\"tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math> is equal to <math alttext=\"tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 2 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>. Since <math alttext=\"StartFraction 2 pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math> is greater than <math alttext=\"StartFraction pi Over 2 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math> and less than <math alttext=\"pi\"><mi>&pi;</mi>\n</math>, it follows that the terminal side of the angle is in quadrant II and forms an angle of <math alttext=\"StartFraction pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext=\"60 degree\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, with the negative <em>x</em>-axis. Therefore, the terminal side of the angle intersects the unit circle at the point <math alttext=\"left parenthesis negative one half comma StartFraction StartRoot 3 EndRoot Over 2 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math>. It follows that&nbsp;the value of <math alttext=\"tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 2 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math> is&nbsp;<math alttext=\"StartStartFraction StartFraction StartRoot 3 EndRoot Over 2 EndFraction OverOver negative one half EndEndFraction\"><mfrac><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mo>-</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mfrac></math>, which is equivalent to <math alttext=\"minus StartRoot 3 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Therefore, the value of <math alttext=\"tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math> is <math alttext=\"minus StartRoot 3 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect. This is the value of <math alttext=\"StartStartFraction 1 OverOver tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis EndEndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></mrow></mfrac></math>, not <math alttext=\"tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. This is the value of <math alttext=\"StartStartFraction 1 OverOver tangent left parenthesis StartFraction pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis EndEndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></mrow></mfrac></math>, not <math alttext=\"tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. This is the value of <math alttext=\"tangent left parenthesis StartFraction pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>, not <math alttext=\"tangent left parenthesis StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mi>tan</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>92</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>.</p>","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">What is the value of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"tangent StartFraction 92 pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>92</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>?</p>","type":"mcq","correct_answer":["A"]},"createDate":1730147357408,"updateDate":1730147357408}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147357408, 1730147357408),
